---
source: crates/surfaces/cli/src/main.rs
expression: command.clone().render_long_help().to_string()
---
List each extension's settings: the value, where it came from, what it gates and each secret's status

`settings set` and `settings unset` write one setting of specforge.json (ADR 0296).

Usage: specforge settings [OPTIONS]
       specforge settings <COMMAND>

Commands:
  set    Write one setting of specforge.json, admitted by its declared type; never a secret
  unset  Remove one setting from specforge.json: it reverts to its preset or default
  help   Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
      --path <PATH>
          A path inside the project (the nearest directory at or above it holding specforge.json; none: the directory itself, with the default configuration)
          
          [default: .]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: human or json (json with --pick)
          
          [default: human]
          [possible values: human, json]

      --pick <PATH>
          Print only the values at this path of the command's JSON answer, one per line, e.g. '.[code=E003].message' on check or '.extensions[].settings[name=<setting>].value' on settings. The path is steps: `.key`, `[n]` (digits, no leading zero), `[]` (every item) and `[<key>=<value>]` (the items whose field <key> is <value>, e.g. `[kind=plan]`: <key> may be dotted, `[fields.result=issue]`, and a number, boolean or null is compared as JSON, `[fields.backlog=true]`, `[fields.wave=2]`; an object or array is never selected); a `.` before `[` may be left out, and `.` alone is the whole answer. One line per value reached: a string bare (a newline in it is printed as is), an integer as is and any other number as an f64 (`1.0`, `-0.0`, `1e3` as `1000.0`), a boolean, object or array as compact JSON; null or a missing value prints nothing, and is not an error. It implies --format json (--format human is refused): errors print as they do under json, and the exit code is the command's.

      --count
          With --pick: print the number of values the path reaches, the lines --pick would print (null or a missing value is none, a string holding a newline is one), 0 for none; nothing when the command answered nothing, so `$(… --count) || n='?'` tells an unread answer from 0. It takes no --first, --last, --join or --fields (ADR 0325 D8).

      --first <N>
          With --pick: keep only the first N values it reaches

      --last <N>
          With --pick: keep only the last N values it reaches

      --join <SEP>
          With --pick: print the values kept on one line joined by SEP exactly as given (no escape is read: $'\n  ' is the shell's), then a newline; nothing when no value is reached. It combines with --first or --last, and takes no --count or --fields (ADR 0325 D8).

      --all
          List the settings nothing sets and that have no default too

      --extension <EXTENSION>
          List only this extension's settings

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')
